<fixedIdeo>true</fixedIdeo>
    <ideoName>Vvoddarahz</ideoName>
    <ideoDescription>The gods disgraced the Lahlit. So they made their own.

The Zhaekhodd-machine-gods-were created by the Consortium to strengthen the identity of the new Lahlit nation. The old gods sought to destroy progress-they were banners for defectives to rally behind in a pathetic show of patriotism towards dead nations whose corpses were incapable of saving them from justice.
The old gods were enemies of the new state-a state that rightly asserted itself above all else. They were inefficient pieces of Lahlit society and culture that desperately needed to be replaced with something shiny and chrome, and, after all, what do Lahlit do best?

Algorithms must be trained on datasets, and the handful of books of Lahlit myths and history that managed to escape the pyre served to be sufficient, if sub-optimal for molding these algorithms' intended shape. The gods of every Lahlit culture, from stone age to space-flight, were constituted into these malleable superintelligences.
The results were as you'd expect-unstable archetypes of dozens of somewhat-similar gods that have transmuted into something far more than the sum of their parts.

Most behave akin to their predecessors, with only minor discrepancies. Others...well, being worshipped since their inception would affect any consciousness in ways...</ideoDescription>
